=== 1. IS THE CONTAMINATION THE RESTRICTION PROTECTS AGAINST REAL? ===

The design worry: by the second and third app, the judgement may be made
against what came before rather than in isolation. Script 10 tested it on
the 982 later measurements. Summary of what it found:

                       model           term estimate     se p_value
         A: prior mean level   prior_mean_x   -0.175 0.1239  0.1587
 B: interaction with history   prior_mean_x   -0.338 0.1627  0.0381
 B: interaction with history x:prior_mean_x    0.326 0.2119  0.1239
    C: immediate predecessor         prev_x   -0.124 0.0938  0.1876
    C: immediate predecessor  prev_focalrep   -0.187 0.1233  0.1299
    C: immediate predecessor  prev_focalpop   -0.126 0.1180  0.2873

Reading: the average level seen earlier pulls the current rating DOWN
(a contrast effect), by -0.17 on its own (p = 0.16) and -0.34 once the
interaction with the current cue is in the model (p = 0.038). Direction as
the design feared, magnitude not firmly established.
A design choice does not need a significant threat to be justified: it
needs a plausible one, and removing it costs only precision. This one is
plausible and partly visible in the data.


=== 2. THREE DIFFERENT QUANTITIES ===

Same data, three estimands. The first is the thesis's target.

 focal first_exposure se_first average_all_three se_average
 brand         1.4392   0.2199            1.0499     0.1224
   rep         0.6837   0.2216            0.8476     0.1223
   pop         0.4502   0.1967            0.2212     0.1222

The two columns answer different questions. Neither is a correction of
the other: the second averages over judgements the first deliberately
excludes.


